@page
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Modules";
}

<h1>Modules</h1>

<p>
    Everything in TypeO is a module: the engine core, 2D support, platform backends and even the
    features of the TypeDitor editor itself. A module is a versioned C# library, described by a
    <code>typeo.json</code> file and distributed through a GitHub repository with version tags.
    This page explains how modules are set up, how you use them in a project, and how to create your own.
</p>

<h2>Using modules in a project</h2>
<p>
    Your game project also has a <code>typeo.json</code>. Its <code>dependencies</code> list names the modules
    (and NuGet packages) it uses. Modules are referenced as <code>Author@Module-version</code>:
</p>
<pre><code>{
  "name": "MyGame",
  "version": "0.1.0",
  "type": "exe",
  "dependencies": [
    "Udrian@TypeOCore-1.1.0",
    "Udrian@TypeOBasic2d-1.0.1",
    "Udrian@TypeODesktop-1.1.0",
    "Udrian@TypeOTK-1.0.0"
  ],
  "project": [
    { "Name": "MyGame", "type": "" }
  ]
}</code></pre>
<p>
    You normally manage this from TypeDitor: add or update a module in the project settings and the editor
    resolves the whole dependency graph, downloads each module version into the local modules cache
    (<code>{cache}/{author}/{name}/{version}</code>), builds them and loads them &mdash; including any
    editor extensions the module ships. The same works from the command line with
    <code>typer dependency -p &lt;project&gt;</code>.
</p>

<h2>How a module is set up</h2>
<p>
    A module repository carries no <code>.csproj</code> or solution files &mdash; only sources plus
    <code>typeo.json</code>, from which <code>typer generate</code> creates the project files. A typical
    module defines up to three kinds of projects:
</p>
<table>
    <thead>
        <tr><th>Project type</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td><code>""</code> (game)</td>
            <td>The runtime library that games reference. Loaded when your game runs.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>"dev"</code> (editor)</td>
            <td>An optional editor extension, loaded by TypeDitor. It can add panels, viewers, tools and
                commands to the editor through the TypeD framework.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>"test"</code></td>
            <td>Unit tests for the module. Never shipped.</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>
<p>As an example, this is a trimmed version of TypeOTK's <code>typeo.json</code>:</p>
<pre><code>{
  "name": "TypeOTK",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "type": "module",
  "externals": [
    "Shaders/tshader.frag",
    "Assets/Icons/**;dev"
  ],
  "dependencies": [
    "Udrian@TypeOCore-1.0.0",
    "Udrian@TypeD-1.0.0;dev",
    "OpenTK-4.9.4;nuget",
    "Avalonia-12.1.4;nuget;dev"
  ],
  "project": [
    { "Name": "TypeOTK",     "type": "" },
    { "Name": "TypeDTK",     "type": "dev",  "depends_on": [ "TypeOTK" ] },
    { "Name": "TypeOTKTest", "type": "test", "depends_on": [ "TypeOTK", "TypeDTK" ] }
  ]
}</code></pre>
<ul>
    <li>
        <strong>Dependencies</strong> &mdash; <code>Author@Module-version</code> references another module;
        adding <code>;nuget</code> makes it a NuGet package reference instead. A trailing <code>;dev</code> or
        <code>;test</code> scopes the dependency to the editor or test project only.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>Externals</strong> &mdash; files (shaders, assets) copied into the consuming project when the
        module is loaded. Untagged entries go to games, <code>;dev</code> entries to the editor,
        <code>;test</code> entries to tests.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>Versioning</strong> &mdash; a module version is simply a git tag like <code>1.0.0</code> on the
        module's repository. Consumers always reference an exact version.
    </li>
</ul>

<h2>Creating your own module</h2>
<ol class="steps">
    <li>
        <strong>Create the module.</strong> Make a new git repository, add <a href="https://github.com/Udrian/typer" rel="noopener">typer</a>
        as a submodule and run <code>typer new</code> (or write <code>typeo.json</code> by hand with
        <code>"type": "module"</code> and your project list as above). Then run
        <code>typer generate -p &lt;path&gt;</code> to create the solution and project files.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>Write the runtime part.</strong> The game project (type <code>""</code>) contains your module
        class and the services, entities or other features games will use. Keep its public API documented with
        XML doc comments &mdash; they become the <a asp-page="/Documentation/Index">generated documentation</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>Optionally extend the editor.</strong> Add a <code>dev</code> project that depends on
        <code>Udrian@TypeD;dev</code>. Its initializer can register panels, viewers, toolbar tools and commands
        when TypeDitor loads the module, and must clean them up again in its uninitializer so the module can be
        hot reloaded.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>Test locally.</strong> In a test game project, add a <code>typeo_override.json</code> that points
        your module name at the local checkout &mdash; TypeDitor then builds and hot reloads it from source while
        you develop (<code>typer addoverride</code> sets this up).
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>Release a version.</strong> Push the repository to GitHub and tag a release, e.g.
        <code>1.0.0</code>. Anyone can now depend on <code>You@YourModule-1.0.0</code>. Tagged versions also show
        up in the <a asp-page="/Documentation/Index">documentation browser</a> once they are indexed.
    </li>
</ol>

<h2>How modules load</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Modules initialize in dependency order &mdash; dependencies first &mdash; and deinitialize in reverse.</li>
    <li>TypeDitor builds modules with the .NET SDK and loads each one in its own collectible load context, which is what makes in-editor hot reload possible.</li>
    <li>External NuGet dependencies are shared across modules, so static state (like GL bindings) stays unified.</li>
    <li>Editor (<code>dev</code>) projects are only ever loaded by TypeDitor; your shipped game loads just the runtime projects.</li>
</ul>
